From 7439f269d198d05774333d966f77c87492f96f44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 13:28:09 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] gc-card-marking: range-check the barrier against the
 collector's window

The barrier indexed the card table with the absolute address, which needed a
table for the whole address space (256 GiB reserved), a start-up hook that
runs before every constructor (.preinit_array: shared libraries do not get
it), and marked garbage for pointers with a tag in their top byte (Android's
allocator tags malloc memory since Android 11).

The collector's memory is now one contiguous window [lo, lo + n * 512), and
the barrier is

    if ((addr - lo) >> 9 < n)  cards[(addr - lo) >> 9] = 1;

An address outside the window (malloc, tagged, an escaped stack slot) costs a
compare and marks nothing; lo, n and the table are zero until the collector
starts, which makes the barrier a no-op until then, so nothing has to run
before the first constructor. Only the first of the two addresses of a
cluster (or the first byte of a small copy) is checked: the second is in the
same object.

The analysis no longer emits code while it walks the function: it records
requests, and the checks are generated afterwards with
SplitBlockAndInsertIfThen, so the basic blocks it iterates are never changed
under it. A copy of unknown size calls __sd_gc_card_range only when its first
byte is inside the window.

Measured (barrier_micro, LDC -O3, medians of 5, pinned): the check costs 0% on
class fields, structs and linked lists, +9% on array element stores
(0.45 to 0.49 ns), +5% to +15% on copies of 8 to 64 pointers. The 500k city
and the 2 and 8 GiB stress tests are the same or slightly better than with
the unchecked barrier.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
